Reference return-path refcount semantics
========================================

When a function returns a `&T`, the refcount accounting at
the return must hand exactly one share to the caller and
release any function-local shares. Cases:

  - **Fresh allocation:** `return new T(...)` — refcount 1
    is already correct; just hand off.

  - **Returning a parameter:** `return r` where `r: &T` is a
    parameter — the parameter had its own share (incremented
    on the way in). On return, the caller's binding needs a
    share; the function-local share must be released. The
    simplest correct lowering is "do nothing extra" — the
    function-local incr at entry survives as the caller's
    share, and the param's "scope-exit decr" is elided
    because ownership transferred.

  - **Returning a field of another ref:** `return p.field`
    where `p: &Outer` and `Outer.field: &Inner`. The returned
    ref isn't owned by the function; the caller needs a fresh
    share, so the return path must increment.

  - **Early return through a branch:** ensures both branches
    do the same accounting; a bug that only fires on one path
    leaks or double-frees.

  - **Recursive return:** stresses the return-path increments
    across multiple call frames.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct NrrBox
        v: int

Fresh allocation in return position
-----------------------------------

    make_box_return(v: int) -> &NrrBox = new NrrBox(v)

    #test
    test_return_fresh() -> unit
        val b1: &NrrBox = make_box_return(1)
        val b2: &NrrBox = make_box_return(2)
        val b3: &NrrBox = make_box_return(3)
        assert(b1.v == 1 && b2.v == 2 && b3.v == 3,
            s"b1=${b1.v} b2=${b2.v} b3=${b3.v}, expected 1 2 3")

Return a borrowed parameter
---------------------------

    identity_ref(b: &NrrBox) -> &NrrBox = b

    #test
    test_return_borrowed_param() -> unit
        val orig: &NrrBox = new NrrBox(50)
        val same: &NrrBox = identity_ref(orig)
        assert(same.v == 50, s"same.v should be 50, got ${same.v}")
        // After identity_ref returns, both bindings still alive
        same.v = 60
        assert(orig.v == 60, s"orig.v should be 60 after same.v=60, got ${orig.v}")

Conditional return: same accounting on every branch
---------------------------------------------------

If the early-return path's refcount accounting differs from the late-
return path, one of them leaks or frees prematurely. Both branches
must hand the caller exactly one share.

    select_box(use_a: bool, a: &NrrBox, b: &NrrBox) -> &NrrBox
        if use_a then return a
        return b

    #test
    test_conditional_return_branches() -> unit
        val a: &NrrBox = new NrrBox(1)
        val b: &NrrBox = new NrrBox(2)
        val pick_a: &NrrBox = select_box(true,  a, b)
        val pick_b: &NrrBox = select_box(false, a, b)
        assert(pick_a.v == 1, s"pick_a should be 1, got ${pick_a.v}")
        assert(pick_b.v == 2, s"pick_b should be 2, got ${pick_b.v}")
        // After both calls, originals must still be live
        assert(a.v == 1, s"a.v should still be 1, got ${a.v}")
        assert(b.v == 2, s"b.v should still be 2, got ${b.v}")

Recursive return
----------------

`build_chain(n)` returns a fresh `&NrrBox` with value `n` by recursing
through the chain. Each recursive call returns a fresh ref; each
parent call drops the child's ref and returns its own. Heavy
allocator + return-path stress.

    build_chain(n: int) -> &NrrBox
        if n == 0 then return new NrrBox(0)
        val _ = build_chain(n - 1)
        return new NrrBox(n)

    #test
    test_recursive_return() -> unit
        val top: &NrrBox = build_chain(20)
        assert(top.v == 20, s"top.v should be 20 after build_chain(20), got ${top.v}")

Discard-then-return doesn't free what we return
-----------------------------------------------

A common pattern: allocate temporaries, throw them away, return the
"real" result. The temporaries' refcounts go to 0 and free; the
returned one must stay alive.

    make_with_scratch() -> &NrrBox
        val _scratch1: &NrrBox = new NrrBox(100)
        val _scratch2: &NrrBox = new NrrBox(200)
        return new NrrBox(300)

    #test
    test_discard_scratch_keep_return() -> unit
        val b: &NrrBox = make_with_scratch()
        assert(b.v == 300, s"b.v should be 300, got ${b.v}")
